WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
Welcome. This class helps you choose an
orchestration pattern because

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.800
the task requires it, not because multiple
agents sound impressive.

3
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:19.200
You will compare direct execution,
deterministic workflows, managers,

4
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:25.600
handoffs, parallel branches, and review
loops while preserving observable routes,

5
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:32.000
budgets, state, and terminal outcomes.

6
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.571
Begin with the simplest structure that
handles the real uncertainty.

7
00:00:37.571 --> 00:00:43.143
If one bounded model call followed by
validation can finish

8
00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:48.714
the task, use it. If the steps and
branches are

9
00:00:48.714 --> 00:00:54.286
already known, encode them in
deterministic application logic. Add

10
00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:59.857
model-directed routing only when selecting
the next step requires semantic

11
00:00:59.857 --> 00:01:05.429
judgment that fixed rules cannot express
economically. For every proposed

12
00:01:05.429 --> 00:01:11.000
agent boundary, write the benefit and the
new cost. A

13
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.571
specialist may reduce prompt conflict,
permission breadth, or evaluation

14
00:01:16.571 --> 00:01:22.143
ambiguity. It also adds latency, state
transfer, another failure surface,

15
00:01:22.143 --> 00:01:27.714
and another place where instructions or
authority can drift. More

16
00:01:27.714 --> 00:01:33.286
agents create more boundaries; they do not
automatically create better

17
00:01:33.286 --> 00:01:38.857
work. If you cannot name the distinct
responsibility, evidence contract,

18
00:01:38.857 --> 00:01:44.429
and terminal behavior of an agent, remove
it or make

19
00:01:44.429 --> 00:01:50.000
it a deterministic function.

20
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.538
Consider a curriculum update. Intake
validates the source record.

21
00:01:55.538 --> 00:02:01.077
Classification chooses direct review,
specialist review, or human escalation. A

22
00:02:01.077 --> 00:02:06.615
specialist produces a typed claim table.
Verification either accepts it,

23
00:02:06.615 --> 00:02:12.154
permits one revision, or escalates. The
application owns this graph.

24
00:02:12.154 --> 00:02:17.692
Models perform bounded nodes; they do not
invent edges. Each

25
00:02:17.692 --> 00:02:23.231
edge records the input version, output
version, route reason, remaining

26
00:02:23.231 --> 00:02:28.769
budget, and next allowed states. A
sequential path handles dependencies.

27
00:02:28.769 --> 00:02:34.308
A conditional path uses validated state. A
bounded loop permits

28
00:02:34.308 --> 00:02:39.846
one measurable improvement. A
deterministic terminal state ends in

29
00:02:39.846 --> 00:02:45.385
accept, escalate, cancel, or fail with
evidence. Google ADK exposes

30
00:02:45.385 --> 00:02:50.923
sequential, loop, and parallel workflow
agents, but the portable principle

31
00:02:50.923 --> 00:02:56.462
is broader: keep known control flow in
code and place

32
00:02:56.462 --> 00:03:02.000
uncertain model work inside explicit
contracts.

33
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Now compare two implementations. Design A
lets an agent decide

34
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:14.000
whether to research, write, verify, retry,
or publish until it

35
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:20.000
feels finished. Design B fixes intake,
research, writing, two independent

36
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.000
verification judgments, human approval,
and publication as governed states. Models

37
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:32.000
choose evidence and language inside
bounded nodes, but cannot skip

38
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.000
approval or invent a terminal state.
Design B exposes failure,

39
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:44.000
cost, and recovery, so it is the reliable
starting point.

40
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.429
Choose a manager when one agent should
retain responsibility for

41
00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:54.857
the user-facing result. The manager calls
specialists as tools, validates

42
00:03:54.857 --> 00:04:00.286
their artifacts, combines evidence,
applies shared guardrails, and owns the

43
00:04:00.286 --> 00:04:05.714
final answer. This centralizes synthesis
and budget control, but it

44
00:04:05.714 --> 00:04:11.143
can become a bottleneck and must be able
to judge

45
00:04:11.143 --> 00:04:16.571
specialist output. Choose a handoff when a
specialist should take

46
00:04:16.571 --> 00:04:22.000
over the next phase under a narrower
instruction set. A

47
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.429
handoff can reduce prompt conflict and
support direct specialist interaction,

48
00:04:27.429 --> 00:04:32.857
but it transfers active control. The
transfer must preserve user

49
00:04:32.857 --> 00:04:38.286
intent, trusted facts, unresolved
questions, allowed actions, completed side

50
00:04:38.286 --> 00:04:43.714
effects, budget, return conditions, and
trace continuity. Delegation returns an

51
00:04:43.714 --> 00:04:49.143
artifact to the manager. A handoff changes
who is active.

52
00:04:49.143 --> 00:04:54.571
Do not use the labels interchangeably, and
do not let

53
00:04:54.571 --> 00:05:00.000
framework syntax decide the product
behavior.

54
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.200
Parallelize only proven independence. Two
branches are not independent when

55
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:10.400
one needs the other's result, both mutate
the same object,

56
00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:15.600
or both consume a shared scarce limit
without coordination. Give

57
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.800
each safe branch its own input snapshot,
scope, permission set,

58
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:26.000
budget, idempotency key, timeout, and
evidence contract. Then design the

59
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.200
join before starting the branches. Name
which results are required,

60
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:36.400
which are optional, how long the join
waits, who cancels

61
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.600
unfinished work, who owns retries, and
what happens when evidence

62
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.800
conflicts. Define whether a partial result
may be shown and

63
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:52.000
how it is labeled. A fast branch does not

64
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.200
authorize a missing required branch. A
late branch does not

65
00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.400
silently overwrite accepted evidence. If
two reviewers disagree, preserve both

66
00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.600
judgments and route to a declared
adjudication or human decision.

67
00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.800
Concurrency is an execution choice; the
join is the product

68
00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:18.000
decision.

69
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:23.143
Make every route reconstructable. Record a
run identifier, parent and

70
00:06:23.143 --> 00:06:28.286
child span, active agent, route reason,
input and output contract

71
00:06:28.286 --> 00:06:33.429
versions, approved tools, decisions,
retries, budget consumption, and terminal

72
00:06:33.429 --> 00:06:38.571
state. Keep secrets and unnecessary
personal data out of telemetry;

73
00:06:38.571 --> 00:06:43.714
retain safe hashes and governed references
when full content is

74
00:06:43.714 --> 00:06:48.857
not appropriate. Bound turns, tokens,
cost, elapsed time, tool calls,

75
00:06:48.857 --> 00:06:54.000
revisions, and recursive transfers. A
limit needs an owner and

76
00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.143
an outcome when exhausted. OpenAI
documents managers and handoffs, Anthropic

77
00:06:59.143 --> 00:07:04.286
describes workflow and agent patterns, and
Google ADK provides workflow

78
00:07:04.286 --> 00:07:09.429
structures. Those are implementation
options, not identical guarantees. Define

79
00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:14.571
product-level invariants first, map each
framework through an adapter, and

80
00:07:14.571 --> 00:07:19.714
run the same route, budget, failure, and
terminal-state tests. Portability

81
00:07:19.714 --> 00:07:24.857
means preserving behavior and evidence,
not pretending every SDK uses

82
00:07:24.857 --> 00:07:30.000
the same objects.

83
00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.000
Choose a real workflow. Identify the one
decision, if any,

84
00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:42.000
that truly requires semantic routing.
Compare a direct call, deterministic

85
00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:48.000
graph, manager, and handoff using quality,
risk, latency, cost, and

86
00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:54.000
debuggability.

87
00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:31.000
Checkpoint. Two proposed branches edit the
same curriculum document. Should

88
00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:38.000
they run concurrently merely because
different models perform the work?

89
00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:43.250
Do not parallelize those writes by
default. Different models do

90
00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:48.500
not make shared state independent.
Serialize the edits, partition the

91
00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:53.750
document into nonoverlapping owned
regions, or have branches return proposals

92
00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:59.000
that a deterministic join reconciles. If
you chose concurrency for

93
00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:04.250
speed, add the missing state and conflict
analysis. If you

94
00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:09.500
rejected all parallel work, refine the
answer: independent read-only evidence

95
00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:14.750
collection may still run concurrently when
the join defines required

96
00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:20.000
results, disagreement, timeout, and
partial failure.

97
00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:27.000
Open the orchestration design activity.
Compare four patterns, create the

98
00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:34.000
smallest typed graph, justify any parallel
branch, define its join,

99
00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:41.000
and simulate normal completion, bad
routing, conflicting evidence, specialist

100
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:48.000
timeout, repeated revision, and human
escalation. Save the graph and

101
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:55.000
six trace records.

102
00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:09.000
When ready, begin the knowledge check. You
will choose deterministic

103
00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:15.000
control for known steps, distinguish
managers from handoffs, reject unsafe

104
00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:21.000
parallel mutation, define explicit joins,
and explain why route reasons

105
00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:27.000
and contract versions belong in traces.

106
00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:32.000
Keep known control flow deterministic,
justify every agent boundary, parallelize

107
00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:37.000
only independence, govern the join, and
make every route and

108
00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:42.000
ending observable.
